  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "narration": "Have you ever wondered how websites check if your password is long enough? They use string methods. These are built-in tools that help us process and change text easily.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TitleCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "String Methods in Java",
          "subtitle": "Mastering text manipulation",
          "badge": "DAY 8 • JAVA STRINGS",
          "tags": [
            "Java",
            "Strings",
            "Methods"
          ]
        },
        "chapter": "M1 Basics"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "narration": "Think of a string as a strip of paper with letters on it. A method is like a specialized tool. One tool counts the letters, while another tool changes them to uppercase.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ArchitectureFlow",
        "props": {
          "title": "What is a Method?",
          "steps": [
            {
              "title": "The String",
              "desc": "Your raw text data, like 'Hello'."
            },
            {
              "title": "The Method",
              "desc": "A tool that performs a specific action."
            },
            {
              "title": "The Result",
              "desc": "The outcome, like the length or new text."
            }
          ],
          "activeStepIndex": 0
        },
        "keyIdea": "Methods are tools for text",
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "tool",
            "zoom": 1.2,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 50
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "narration": "Why do we need these? Imagine checking if a user's email contains an at-symbol manually. It would take many lines of code. Methods do this work in just one simple step.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "SummaryList",
        "props": {
          "title": "Why Use Methods?",
          "items": [
            "Saves time and effort",
            "Makes code cleaner and readable",
            "Handles complex logic automatically",
            "Prevents manual counting errors"
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "manually",
            "text": "Don't reinvent the wheel!",
            "kind": "tip"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "narration": "Let us look at the process. We take a string, call a method using a dot, and get an answer. This flow is the same for almost every method we will use today.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "DiagramFlow",
        "props": {
          "title": "The Method Call Flow",
          "nodes": [
            {
              "id": "str",
              "label": "String Variable"
            },
            {
              "id": "dot",
              "label": "."
            },
            {
              "id": "meth",
              "label": "Method Name()"
            },
            {
              "id": "res",
              "label": "Result"
            }
          ],
          "edges": [
            {
              "from": "str",
              "to": "dot"
            },
            {
              "from": "dot",
              "to": "meth"
            },
            {
              "from": "meth",
              "to": "res"
            }
          ],
          "steps": [
            {
              "at": "string",
              "label": "Start with text",
              "focus": [
                "str"
              ]
            },
            {
              "at": "dot",
              "label": "Use the dot operator",
              "arrow": 1
            },
            {
              "at": "answer",
              "label": "Get the output",
              "focus": [
                "res"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "narration": "Our first tool is length. It counts every character in the string, including spaces. Look at this code. We create a string and then ask for its length using the length method.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "Look",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Main.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "String text = \"Java Code\";\nint size = text.length();\nSystem.out.println(size);",
          "highlightLines": [
            2
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "length",
            "zoom": 1.7,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 35
          }
        ],
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "spaces",
            "text": "Spaces are counted too!",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "narration": "When we run this, the output is nine. Why? Because J-a-v-a is four, the space is five, and C-o-d-e makes it nine. The computer counts everything inside the quotes.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TerminalRun",
        "props": {
          "command": "java Main.java",
          "output": [
            "9"
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "nine",
            "zoom": 1.5,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 50
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "narration": "Next, we have case conversion. The to-upper-case method turns everything into capital letters. The to-lower-case method does the opposite. This is very useful for comparing user input.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "Next",
        "props": {
          "filename": "CaseChange.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "String msg = \"Hello\";\nSystem.out.println(msg.toUpperCase());\nSystem.out.println(msg.toLowerCase());",
          "highlightLines": [
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        "keyIdea": "toUpperCase and toLowerCase"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "narration": "Watch the output. The first line prints HELLO in all caps. The second line prints hello in small letters. Note that the original message variable remains unchanged.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TerminalRun",
        "props": {
          "command": "java CaseChange.java",
          "output": [
            "HELLO",
            "hello"
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "unchanged",
            "text": "Strings are immutable!",
            "kind": "tip"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "narration": "Now let us check for specific words. The contains method returns true if the text is found. It is like a search tool. Here, we check if the word Java is present.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "Now",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Search.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "String goal = \"Learn Java\";\nboolean hasJava = goal.contains(\"Java\");\nSystem.out.println(hasJava);",
          "highlightLines": [
            2
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "contains",
            "zoom": 1.8,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 35
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "narration": "The output is true. If we searched for Python, it would return false. Remember, this method is case-sensitive. Searching for lowercase java would also return false.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TerminalRun",
        "props": {
          "command": "java Search.java",
          "output": [
            "true"
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "case-sensitive",
            "text": "J is not the same as j",
            "kind": "note"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "narration": "What if we want to swap words? The replace method finds a target and swaps it with something else. It creates a brand new string with the changes applied.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "ComparisonCard",
        "props": {
          "title": "The Replace Method",
          "leftTitle": "Original",
          "leftPoints": [
            "String: 'I like Tea'",
            "Target: 'Tea'",
            "Replacement: 'Coffee'"
          ],
          "rightTitle": "New String",
          "rightPoints": [
            "Result: 'I like Coffee'",
            "Original stays same",
            "Very useful for fixing typos"
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 12,
      "narration": "A common mistake beginners make is forgetting the parentheses after the method name. In Java, length for a string is a method, so it must have brackets. Without them, the code will not run.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "TypingCode",
        "type_anchor": "forgetting",
        "props": {
          "filename": "Error.java",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "String s = \"Hi\";\n// Error: missing ()\nint len = s.length;\n// Correct way:\nint correctLen = s.length();",
          "highlightLines": [
            3
          ]
        },
        "popups": [
          {
            "anchor": "brackets",
            "text": "Methods always need ()",
            "kind": "tip"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 13,
      "narration": "To recap, use length to count, to-upper-case to capitalize, and contains to search. These tools make text handling easy. Practice these today to get comfortable.",
      "remotion": {
        "type": "SummaryList",
        "props": {
          "title": "Key Takeaways",
          "items": [
            "length() counts characters and spaces",
            "toUpperCase() and toLowerCase() change case",
            "contains() checks if text exists",
            "Methods always end with parentheses ()"
          ]
        },
        "camera": [
          {
            "at": "recap",
            "zoom": 1.0,
            "x": 50,
            "y": 50
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": 14,
